  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stays put, gains an injected [38;5;153mfetch[39m, and tests boot a fake DOM.
  [3mBuys:[0m no file moves, no [38;5;153mindex.html[39m change.
  [3mCosts:[0m adds jsdom — your first dependency, in a repo that currently has zero. Tests get slower and test the DOM as a
  side effect of testing auth. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m keeps mixing network, validation, and DOM in one file.

  [1mC. Extract [38;5;153mauth.js[39m as a dual CommonJS/browser-global shim[0m
  [38;5;153mauth.js[39m assigns to [38;5;153mwindow[39m in a browser and [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m under Node, via a conditional footer.
  [3mBuys:[0m plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m tags keep working, [38;5;153mfile://[39m keeps working, tests are CommonJS.
  [3mCosts:[0m the shim is hand-rolled machinery that exists only to satisfy two loaders, and it's easy to get subtly wrong.
  Global namespace pollution.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It's the only one that separates concerns [3mand[0m adds no dependencies, and the [38;5;153m.mjs[39m extension sidesteps
  the [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m collision with [38;5;153msrc/[39m that would otherwise force renaming those files too.

  [1mTwo things I want to flag before you choose — neither changes the recommendation:[0m

  1. [1mThis is a security fix, not just a feature.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m currently returns [38;5;153m{success: true}[39m for [3many[0m input. The app
     authenticates nobody. Making it real is the substantive change.
  2. [1mOut of scope, but you'll hit it next:[0m there's no session concept here. Once a real [38;5;153muserId[39m comes back, nothing holds
     it or uses it. A [38;5;153muserId[39m is an identifier, not a credential — it can't authorize anything. I'd keep session/token
     handling out of this spec and do it as separate work.
